This is the README for asm80.

This is an assembler for the 8080 processor. 
asm80 is a two pass assembler. The first pass
determines addresses. The second pass does the
actual assembly.

asm80 uses standard Intel 8080 opcodes defined 
in the Intel MCS-80 book published in the 1970's. 
asm80 compiles most CP/M era source code programs 
with a few minor differences:

1. This is NOT a macro assembler. 
2. Quoted strings (ie in DB statements) must
use "double qoutes", NOT 'single quotes'. You may
need to change these by hand if your program
uses single quotes.
3. IF/ELSE/ENDIF and IFDEF/IFUNDEF statements
are not implimented. You will need to make changes
to your program if it uses conditional assembly.

To make asm80:
$> make

Then copy it to your bin directory:
$> sudo cp asm80 /usr/local/bin/


There are 3 basic rules:
1. Labels start in column 0. 
2. Everything else (opcodes, org, DB/DW/DS)
starts after column 0. 
3. Comments (;comments) start wherever they want.


Directives are:
------------------------------------------
EQU - equates. Can be UPPER or lower case.

CR	EQU	13
LF	EQU	10
TLA	equ	0xff0
TLAF	equ	TLA+1
TLAD	EQU	TLA+100h

------------------------------------------------------
ORG - set program address. Can be upper or lower case.
The org directive must start after column 0.

START	ORG	0h
	org 100

--------------------------------------------------------
DB - Define Byte. Store byte sized data in your program.
DB can be UPPER case or lower case.
Strings are surrounded by double quotes "string"
Single characters are surrounded by single quotes '$' 

MSG1	DB "This is a string",CR,LF,0
PROMPT	db "> "
debug	DB	CBh		;; custom opcode for processor
stor1	db	0
stor2	db	0
stor3	DB	0

-----------------------------------------------------------
DW - Define Word. Set up space for a single word (2 bytes).
DW can be UPPER case or lower case.

ADR1	DW	0		; 2 bytes are reserved for ADR1
ADR2	DW	1234	; 2 bytes are reserved for ADR2


DS - Define Storage. Reserve space for a block of bytes.
DS can be UPPER case or lower case.

BUFFER	DS 80		; reserve 80 bytes for a text buffer


--------------------
; This is a comment.
Comments start with a single semi-colon ';' and end
at end of line.

-------------------------------------------------------
Labels start at column 0 and can be UPPER or lower case
and may have MixEdCasE and numbers123. 
Labels have a max length of 40 characters. Any duplication
will be flagged as an error.
A label can have a colon ':' as the final character to match
the syntax older CP/M assemblers has. The colon will be stripped
off and ignored. Special characters '_ - !' etc CANNOT be used
in a label. ONLY Alpha Numeric characters [0-9] [Aa-Zz].
There is a maximum of 1000 labels per program.

--------------------------------------------
Opcodes are pulled from Intel's MCS-80 book.
Opcodes may be UPPER and lower case.
Two opcodes: lxi and mvi may have trailing commas:

	LXI SP,STACK
	mvi a,01h
	mvi b,	'%'
	lxh h	BUFFER

-----------------------------------------------------
Numbers can be decimal, 4 digit hex with a trailing h
or 4 digit starting with 0x.

TCB	EQU	ffh	; 2 digit hex
STORE1	EQU	100	; decimal
STACK	EQU	0x1000	; 4 digit hex


==========================================

Any error encountered will display the error, the line number
and the line of the program. Assembly will stop on any error.
After fixing the error, restart the assemble process.

After assembly, three files will be generated:

filename.com	A binary file in normal order. Should be 
		runnable on a CP/M system or any system
		that runs 8080 object code.

filename.prn	A printable listing used for debugging.

filename.hex	An Intel HEX file suitable for uploading.

===========================================


------------------
A typical session:
------------------
Use a text editor to create t4.asm:

$> vi t4.asm
; t4.asm - a simple 8080 assembler program

CR	EQU	0dh
LF	EQU	0ah
CONOUT	EQU	255	; port 255 decimal for my system

	org 0h		; set start address
start	LXI SP,STACK	; load the stack pointer. The start label 
			; is not needed, just good practice.
	LXI H	MSG1	; point to a message to display
	CALL	PRINT	; show message
	HLT		; stop the program

PRINT	; display a message on the terminal
	MOV A,M		; no space between A and ,M
	CPI	0	; NULL terminates the string
	RZ		; return on zero match
	OUT	CONOUT
	INX	H
	JMP	PRINT	; continue until NULL

; Program Storage
MSG1	DB	"Hello, World!",CR,LF,0
SPACE	DS	32	; give the stack 32 bytes to play with
STACK

---------------------------
Compile the program t4.asm:
---------------------------

$> ASM80 t4.asm
PASS 1
PASS 2
Assembly complete. 
Hex file Written

---------------------
